## 612-660 · 2026-11-04 · Fix B adjudication (replay gated → keep durable row) and Part 1 closure; H1 census started
I completed the Fix B adjudication that the prior compartment left mid-investigation. The question: does any LKG replay consumer use the durable row UNCONDITIONALLY (serve without validating the slot corresponds to current derived state), or is replay gated on freshness/identity? I enumerated every durable-row consumer fresh at source — read `lkg-replay.ts`, the `transform.ts` drop chain, checked build-refusal reasons, and examined the Pi mirror of the drop chain. The ruling: replay is fully gated. Therefore the durable row is KEPT on `no_anchor`/`missing_ids`-class build refusals (logged `lkg_kept reason=no_reanchor`), not dropped. This prevents the churn cascade (bustsServedPrefix → dropSlot → next boot served `lkg=absent`).

Implementation: seam refactor (behavior-preserving `return "drop"` extraction), discriminated union for capture outcome so `!captured` narrows, keep/divergence laws written red-first then green. Pi's lane is structurally immune (its drop requires a computed-changed candidate). Recorded the §25.2 ruling in the design doc and committed. Part 1 complete (4 commits total: Fix A TS, Fix A Pi, comment fixes, Fix B).

Then I started H1 (intra-blessed-file behavior census): verified live counts across the census targets, confirmed all live shapes, and began implementation.

## 661-720 · Fix C: pending_growth arm ruled redundant and deleted; anti-vacuity law generalized to all inputs
Coordinator dispatched Fix C — a second latent storm of the same class as Fix A, unmasked by the docs hotpatch. Field evidence: lane-agent session receipts 04:10:06→04:12:35, `reason=pending_growth` rotate+execute every boundary pass, identical bytes=67,272 (then 69,790 after the legit max_compartment_seq fold). Root cause at `transform-postprocess-phase.ts:1781-1782`: the promotion call site passes `pendingBytes: input.storedRegion.length` — the ABSOLUTE region size — into the pending-growth arm, while the design specifies the NOT-YET-VISIBLE DELTA vs the m[1] cap (~20% history budget) WITH a small-region floor. The floor is omitted entirely at the call site. Result: every session whose region exceeds (historyBudgetTokens*0.2*4) ≈ 36.7KB storms pending_growth on every execute-band pass. The docs trigger was masking this arm because it returned first in the same loop.

I read the call site, the decider arm, and the head-lane precedent at `inject-compartments.ts:4272-4305` (how delta bytes were computed pre-region). Censused `pending_growth` usage across both lanes — both carry the defect. Adjudicated semantics: the arm is REDUNDANT. The four DELTA markers (maxMemoryId = memories past watermark, maxCompartmentSeq = unpublished compartments, profileVersion, docs hash) already measure exactly those deltas; with markers correctly wired post-Fix-A, pending_growth adds only the large-mutation-count aspect, which is not a distinct rotation trigger. Ruled to delete the arm.

U: "pin EVERY input of decideSystemRegionRotation to production sourcing at the call site, not just currentMarkers — the unit-level system-region-rotation.test.ts `pressure` fixture passed while production passed region bytes as queue delta"

Implemented the deletion across six surfaces: removed the pending_growth arm from the decider, removed the `historyBudgetTokens` argument from the caller, rewrote unit pressure fixtures as redundancy laws (asserting the arm's absence rather than its behavior), added the Pi mirror law. Red-first confirmed both lanes before green. Hit a self-inflicted issue: my Pi comment reintroduced the banned token `pending_growth` — rephrased to keep the law strict.

Field context (coordinator-verified, not re-investigated): installed ctxv16 hotpatched a second time (`pendingBytes: 0` at both wiring chunks — arm inert in the field; registry 101be39, memory #2711), host restarted, boots warm, zero storm receipts.
